#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:165
msgid "source:access_point_type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:170
msgid "The method by which an access point to a source has been created, such as by page or archive snapshot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:175
msgid "Text, controlled, single entry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:190
msgid "``page``, ``frame``, ``clip``, ``archive``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:195
msgid "A source has at least one access point, but may have many. For example, if a source is a document we may draw information from a number of different pages (or range of pages). For each page or range of pages, we would create a new access point to the source. The field :ref:`source:access_point_type` tells us what method we have used to create the access point - in this case ``page``. The number of the page or page range will be recorded in a related attribute called :ref:`source:access_point_trigger`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:197
msgid "There are seven ways to \"trigger\" a citation of a specific source, taking into account the source's media type:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:199
msgid "``archive``: an archive snapshot of the source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:200
msgid "``page``: a page or range of pages in a document source like a book or report."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:201
msgid "``line``: a line or range of lines in a line-numbered document like an interview transcript."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:202
msgid "``clip``: a passage from a video or audio source, comprising a start time and a stop time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:203
msgid "``frame``: a single capture point in a video."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:204
msgid "``paragraph``: where a document is numbered throughout, such as in United Nations Security Council documents, paragraphs can be used as access point triggers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:205
msgid "``cell``: the cell reference or range within a table of data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:207
msgid "As we seek to include different types of sources in our work, we anticipate updating this list of citation types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:211
msgid "source:access_point_trigger"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:216
msgid "A set of different types of values that describe where in a source to find the exact content that comprises the citation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:219
msgid "Type of field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:221
msgid "Number, number range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:236
msgid "``11``, ``11-12``, ``11,13``, ``11,13,14-19``, ``1:31-1:40``, ``3(1)(c)``, ``1.1.1-2.4.1``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:242
msgid "This field is used to specify the exact content within a source that defines the citation. For example, if we want to create a citation of page 4 of a source then we would set the value in :ref:`source:access_point_type` to ``page`` and enter ``4`` in this attribute. As noted in the documentation for :ref:`source:access_point_type` there are seven ways to trigger a citation. These are listed below, along with the data type and format required to specify the exact content of the citation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:244
msgid "``archive``: Leave empty. The value in :ref:`source:archive_url` serves as the trigger for this citation type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:245
msgid "``page``: Single page (``1``), single range of pages (``1-2``), combination of page and page ranges (``1,2-3,4,5-8``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:246
msgid "``line``: Single line (``200``), single range of lines (``200-230``), combination of line and line ranges (``200-230,236,240-250``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:247
msgid "``clip``: Single range containing start and end time in the format ``hh:mm:ss`` (``00:01:20-00:01:24``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:248
msgid "``frame``: A single capture point from a video in ``hh:mm:ss`` format (``00:01:20``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:249
msgid "``paragraph``: if a document has numbered paragraphs in any format (e.g. ``3(1)(a)``), they can be captured here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:250
msgid "``cell``: the grid reference (``C123``) of the cell, or cell range (``C123-C129``), containing the data used to evidence the claim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:252
msgid "The range of access point triggers may extend as different media forms become available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:256
msgid "source:accessed_timestamp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:261
msgid "Full date on which the researcher created this citation from the source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:266
msgid "Date formatted as``YYYY-MM-DD``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:281
msgid "``2022-02-20``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:286
msgid "When a Staff Researcher accesses a source in order to create a citation, they should record the full, exact date in this attribute. This data is a useful part of quality assurance processes, enabling us to re-visit sources at set points in time to assess whether they have been updated. Recording an access date at the level of the citation also reflects that we may work on some sources for extended periods of time, or work on the same source at different points in time during our research."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:290
msgid "source:archive_url"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:295
msgid "URL of a snapshot of the source captured by the Internet Archive and hosted on its Wayback Machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:300
msgid "String formatted as URL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:315
msgid "``https://web.archive.org/web/20150703120013/http://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/AFR44/043/2012/en/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:320
msgid "A source becomes usable by Staff Researchers when it has a citation. After entering the source's basic details (like :ref:`source:title`), the researcher can create a first citation by specifying an Internet Archive snapshot to use. If the source is not already archived in the Internet Archive, the researcher should attempt to create a new snapshot to use as the citation. Where snapshots for the source already exist in the Internet Archive, the Staff Researcher should find the snapshot that is earliest in time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:322
msgid "In the majority of cases, this will suffice. However, in some cases, we may need to specify more than one Internet Archive snapshot for the same source - each different snapshot creates a distinct citation. The common reason for this is that the source content changes, but the basic source level details such as the title or url of the source do not. A good example of this is this (dead) URL published by the *Secretaría de la Defensa Nacional* in Mexico: ``http://www.sedena.gob.mx:80/ejercito/comandancias/gur_mil.htm``. It lists the commanders of Mexico's military garrisons, and we have included reference to this in our data about the Mexican army. The title, initial publication date, publication and basic URL did not change: however, over time the content did. In each of 24 different snapshots made by the Internet Archive, the list of commanders is different. In this case, we have a single source with 24 citations: each citation refers to a specific version of that source containing the exact information that we relied upon to create various claims."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:324
msgid "The example above also illustrates an important point: sometimes a source is only available in an archived form, because its original source URL is no longer online. There are many reasons a link may no longer be live, and this problem is known as \"linkrot\". In these cases, the researcher can fill in :ref:`source:url` with a portion of the Internet Archive URL printed after the timestamp. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:326
msgid "Archive URL: ``https://web.archive.org/web/20040208204841/http://www.sedena.gob.mx:80/ejercito/comandancias/gur_mil.htm``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:327
msgid "Original URL extracted from the Archive URL: ``http://www.sedena.gob.mx:80/ejercito/comandancias/gur_mil.htm``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:331
msgid "source:archive_timestamp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:336
msgid "Timestamp of the Internet Archive snapshot that has been used to create a citation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:341
msgid "Date (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:351
msgid ":citation/archived-at"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:356
msgid "``2004-02-08T20:48:41Z``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:361
msgid "Every snapshot made by the Internet Archive contains a timestamp of the time (GMT/UTC) when that snapshot was created. The timestamp is contained in the URL and looks like this: ``20040208204841``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:363
msgid "We extract this part of the URL and reformat it to something more human readable (an ISO 8601 format): ``2004-02-08T20:48:41Z``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/source-citation-attributes.rst:365
msgid "The timestamp provides useful quality assurance data."
msgstr ""
